own 11 live "parallel-loop-mandate — autonomous-loop work MUST parallelize when possible (sub-agent fan-out, multi-task dispatch, cross-repo trawl)"
  slug parallel-loop-mandate
  base anima autonomous-loop-dynamic execution efficiency
  since 2026-04-28 (user directive: 'raw 에 루프 관련 strengthen 필요, 병렬 진행가능한 부분은 병렬로 무조건 진행하도록')
  scope every autonomous-loop-dynamic iter + cron-mode iter + sub-agent dispatch session in anima context. Includes 4-axis sub-agent kicks (raw 47 cross-repo-trawl-witness pattern), parallel hexa tool runs across independent corpora, multi-vendor GPU dispatch (RunPod + vast.ai per own 6), Conway grid+density sweep parallel scans, multi-cycle iter parallelization where dependencies absent.
  rule when ≥2 forward steps are mutually independent (no data/timing dependencies), parallel execution is MANDATORY (not optional). Sequential-when-parallel-feasible = anti-pattern. Single Agent tool call MUST batch multiple independent agents. Single Bash call MUST chain independent commands via & or background. own 5 (no concurrent count limit) authorizes unbounded parallel; own 6 (multi-vendor GPU) authorizes cross-vendor parallel.
  override sequential-default-bias — when in doubt about dependencies, START parallel + abort if dependency emerges (cheaper than waste of serialization)
  preserve raw 25 concurrent-git-lock — git operations still serialize via exp-backoff (raw 25 mandate stands)
  preserve raw 78 daemon-singleton-stale-lock — single-instance-per-host orchestration safety (parallel applies to compute, not orchestrator state)
  preserve raw 86 cost-attribution — every parallel branch emits cost-center + task-id (no aggregation hiding)
  preserve raw 91 honesty triad — each parallel result reports independent verdict, no cross-contamination of caveats
  exception cross-iter dependency (e.g., #117 atom-prep needs #115 P4 sweep results) — explicit sequential mandate. Per-cycle dependency declared in cycle plan.
  exception single-task-cost > $5 fan-out — agent decides if parallel-N pods worth canary-probe per pod first
  why anima research-completeness mandate (own 5) requires parallel speedup. Sequential-default wastes wallclock that compounds across iter count. Sub-agent kick pattern (raw 47 + raw 105) already supports parallel; this rule makes it MANDATORY default. Cron-mode + autonomous-loop iter both benefit — single iter can deliver 4× substantive work via 4-agent parallel fan-out vs 4 sequential iters.
  why example 1: anima-cmd-loop session 2026-04-28 4-agent kick rounds 1-4 (commits 062de44a + 6b28bc49 + c8ed96d7 + ...) delivered 4× substantive findings simultaneously. Same work sequentially would take 4× wallclock with high context-window cost.
  why example 2: Conway 5x5 + 10x10 + 20x20 grid sweep could parallelize (3 hexa subprocess), but session ran sequentially (iter 17-19) — opportunity cost ~3× wallclock. Future similar sweeps MUST batch.
  why example 3: vast.ai 4-tool dispatch family (commits 3ba4ab05 + 61532a3c + c756d911 + d3fb3120) could fire all 4 in parallel (own 5 + own 6 authorize) — total wallclock min(per-pod) instead of sum(per-pod).
  proof commit 062de44a + 6b28bc49 + c8ed96d7 + a945dcd0 + 86640088 (all 4 sub-agent commits within 1 round, single message)
  proof tool/anima_canonical_helper_lock.hexa registered list 16 helpers (multi-tool dispatch unit)
  follow-up audit existing autonomous-loop iters for sequential-when-parallel-feasible anti-pattern (retroactive analysis)
  follow-up integrate own 11 enforcement into nexus kick / autonomous-loop selftest (--check-parallelism flag)
